@startuml "Bridge - Window (Solution) - Class Diagram"
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
title After — the platform is an object the window holds (the solution)


abstract class Window <<Abstraction>> {
  - imp : WindowImp
  + drawRect(..) : void
  + drawText(..) : void
  + drawBorder() : void
  + drawContents() : void
  + setImp(WindowImp) : void
}

class IconWindow <<RefinedAbstraction>> {
  + drawContents() : void
}
class TransientWindow <<RefinedAbstraction>> {
  + drawContents() : void
}

interface WindowImp <<Implementor>> {
  + deviceRect(..) : void
  + deviceText(..) : void
  + deviceRaise() : void
  + deviceLower() : void
}

class XWindowImp <<ConcreteImplementor>> {
  + deviceRect(..) : void
  + deviceText(..) : void
}
class PMWindowImp <<ConcreteImplementor>> {
  + deviceRect(..) : void
  + deviceText(..) : void
}

Window o--> "imp" WindowImp
Window <|-- IconWindow
Window <|-- TransientWindow
WindowImp <|.. XWindowImp
WindowImp <|.. PMWindowImp

note right of WindowImp
  <b>Primitives only.</b>
  deviceRect, deviceText — the smallest
  things a window system can be asked.
  There is no deviceIcon here, and if
  one appeared the two hierarchies would
  have grown back together.
end note

note bottom of PMWindowImp
  PM has no rectangle call, so it
  builds one from a polyline.
  The window never finds out.
end note

note as N1
  <b>Two hierarchies, growing independently.</b>

     3 window kinds  +  2 platforms  =  5 classes
     a 3rd platform  ->  6        a 4th kind -> 6

  And the link is a field, so `window.setImp(pm)`
  moves a window that <b>already exists</b> to another
  platform — which the design on the left cannot do
  at any price.
end note

@enduml
